---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const diffHref = toolPathBySlug('date-difference-calculator');
const ageHref = toolPathBySlug('age-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>What time should you go to bed? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Count whole sleep cycles back from the alarm, and put the minutes it takes to fall asleep
      in front of the first cycle. A common cycle is 90 minutes. A common gap before sleep starts
      is about 15 minutes. For a 7:00 am alarm, five cycles and that 15 minute gap put you in bed
      at 11:15 pm. Six cycles put you in bed at 9:45 pm. Four cycles land at 12:45 am. Three land
      at 2:15 am.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those four times are the whole answer this sleep cycle calculator is for. Pick the one that
      matches how long you can actually stay in bed. The page also runs the other way: type when
      you get into bed, and it lists wake times that finish a cycle. Both directions use the same
      two numbers, the cycle length and the fall-asleep gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      A chat reply can recite "90 minutes." It will not keep the gap, the cycle, and both
      directions on one screen while you change the alarm. The arithmetic stays in the browser.
      Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Sleep Cycle Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="cycles">
    <h2>What is a 90 minute sleep cycle? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      One cycle is a pass through lighter sleep, deeper sleep, and REM, and then back toward
      lighter sleep. Textbooks often draw that pass as about 90 minutes in an adult. The length
      is an average, not a law. The first cycle of a night is often shorter. Later cycles often
      carry more REM. A page of clock times cannot see which kind of sleep you are in.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the clock can do is avoid the middle. Many people find that waking near the end of a cycle feels easier
      than waking from deep sleep, where grogginess, called sleep inertia, tends to be stronger.
      That idea, not a measurement of you, is why a bedtime list exists at all. The list is 6, 5, 4, and 3 cycles because those are the lengths people actually
      choose: a long night, a full night, a short night, and a very short one. At 90 minutes,
      the sleep itself is 9 hours, 7.5 hours, 6 hours, or 4.5 hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cycle field accepts 60 to 120 minutes. If you have a reason to try 80 or 100, type it.
      Every time on the page moves. The page will not tell you that 90 was medically right, and
      it will not tell you that your new number is either. It only keeps the arithmetic honest.
    </p>
    <p>
      A bedtime calculator has one job: a clock time in, four clock times out, with the
      fall-asleep minutes kept separate from the cycles. A list that only prints one "ideal" bedtime is hiding the nights
      you can actually keep. Four rows make the trade visible before you set the alarm.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="latency">
    <h2>Why is time to fall asleep outside the cycle? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Getting into bed is not the start of cycle one. Most people lie there for a while. If you
      subtract only multiples of 90 from a 7:00 am alarm, you are planning to be asleep at 10:00
      pm for six cycles, or at 11:30 pm for five. You are not planning the moment you turn the
      light off. Sleep then starts about 15 minutes late, so the alarm rings about 15 minutes before the last
      cycle ends, which is the thing the list was supposed to avoid.
    </p>
    <p>
      Put the gap in the other box. Fifteen minutes is the default because it is a common
      allowance, not because it was measured on you. Change it. At 15 minutes and 90 minute
      cycles, six cycles before 7:00 am means in bed at 9:45 pm, asleep around 10:00 pm, awake
      at 7:00 am. The extra quarter hour is time in bed. It is not a seventh fragment of a cycle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Zero is legal, and it is the case the page warns about. The caution says the list assumes
      instant sleep. Add a realistic number and the caution leaves. The clock times stay. That
      warning is the whole point of the field: a lights-out countdown hides the gap, and this
      one names it when you have set the gap to nothing.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The gap is not a cycle">
      <p>
        Minutes to fall asleep sit before cycle one. Folding them into the 90 minutes is how an
        alarm that "lands on a cycle" still feels early.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="directions">
    <h2>How do bedtime and wake time differ? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      "When I wake" counts backward. You type the alarm. The results are bedtimes: when to get
      into bed so that sleep, after the gap, contains a whole number of cycles. "When I go to
      bed" counts forward. You type when you get into bed. The results are wake times. Same
      formula, opposite direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Try 10:30 pm, 15 minutes to fall asleep, and 90 minute cycles. Five cycles wake you at
      6:15 am. You are asleep around 10:45 pm, then 7.5 hours later it is 6:15 am. Six cycles
      from the same bedtime wake you at 7:45 am. The page prints both, plus four cycles and
      three, so you can see the shorter options without doing the addition again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clock text can be 7:00 am, 07:00, or 19:00. 7:00 pm and 19:00 are the same minute. 12:00 am
      is midnight. If the text is not a time, the page asks again instead of guessing a nearby
      hour. That keeps a typo from shifting the whole night by twelve hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, set the alarm to 7:00 am, keep 15 minutes to fall asleep, and leave the cycle
      at 90. The five-cycle row says 11:15 pm in bed. Switch to "When I go to bed", type 11:15
      pm, and the five-cycle wake time is 7:00 am again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Starting the first cycle at lights-out">
      <p>
        Six times 90 minutes before 7:00 am is 10:00 pm asleep, not 10:00 pm in bed. Subtract
        the fall-asleep minutes as well, or the alarm lands inside the last cycle.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Leaving the gap at zero">
      <p>
        Zero is a switch, not a measurement. The caution is there so a blank plan does not look
        like a careful one. Put in the number you actually need.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Treating five cycles as a prescription">
      <p>
        7.5 hours is one row on the list. If you need to be up for a shift, four cycles may be
        the time you can keep. The page shows the clock. It does not rank your health.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="This is not a sleep study">
      <p>
        No wearable, no score, no diagnosis. If short nights persist across these times, that
        question belongs with a clinician. The tool only lines up the clock.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A bedtime is a clock problem. A span of calendar days is a different one, and it belongs
      in the <a href={diffHref}>date difference calculator</a>. An exact age from a birth date
      belongs in the <a href={ageHref}>age calculator</a>. Neither of those reads a sleep cycle,
      and this page does not try to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Come back when the alarm changes. The same 15 minutes and the same 90 minute cycle produce
      a new row of times in one edit. Leave the gap filled in unless you truly fall asleep at once.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Check a 7:00 am alarm →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
